{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":2,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576026764,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50Mjg3MjYxMzExMQ=="},"title":"\"datalad status\" no return value when nothing to commit","was":"\"datalad status\" no terminal output for clean datasets"},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576030037,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI4NzI2OTgyMTA="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576053928,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NDQzODkzOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3916#issuecomment-564438939"},"message":"I am not convinced that rendering a message is needed or helpful. Being silent is a common behavior IMHO.\n\n```\nmih@meiner ~ % mkdir empty\nmih@meiner ~ % ls empty\nmih@meiner ~ %\n```\nIt doesn't say \"there is nothing here\" ;-)\n\nThere are also other reasons for running `status`, besides an intention to `save` something. Also,  it is not entirely correct that there is no \"return value\". In fact status reports verbosely. You can enable that via something like: `datalad -f json_pp status`. It is just the behavior of the default result renderer to be silent, when everything is clean.\n\nI hope that sheds some light on the situation.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576098271,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NDczMTg1OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3916#issuecomment-564731858"},"message":"Mine was a suggestion, of course. But from a development perspective I can hardly think of any tool that implies the work is done by not printing anything on screen. In addition, the `ls` command return the prompt instantly if there are no files, while `datalad status` can sit for 10 minutes and then return nothing. The first is a linux command with a single well defined purpose, whereas `datalad status` is a full algorithm of checks on git, git-annex, symlinks, etc. It just looks weird that the user request to know the status of a dataset gives no response at all, even more so since the underlying tools produce a response for the same `status` call.\n\nI checked `datalad -f json_pp status` and, honestly, I don't think is of any use for routine use. It just prints a huge list of information that is not informative for humans. One would need to check by eye if the two commits match. Even trying to filter the output with ` datalad -f json_pp status | grep status\" prints a huge list that brings no information.\n\nJust my view on interfacing tools with a user. Maybe it would be helpful to add a flag on the level of information, i.e., say something at the end, or show some verbosity on what steps are being performed during a, say 10 minute long, run.\n\nThis is what `datalad -f json_pp status` produces on the containers dataset.\n\n```\n{\n  \"action\": \"status\",\n  \"bytesize\": 10344,\n  \"gitshasum\": \"fc74657269fdde51a30036a90a034ee72b55983b\",\n  \"parentds\": \"/DATA/FEDERATED_DATASETS/containers\",\n  \"path\": \"/DATA/FEDERATED_DATASETS/containers/scripts/create_singularities\",\n  \"prev_gitshasum\": \"fc74657269fdde51a30036a90a034ee72b55983b\",\n  \"refds\": \"/DATA/FEDERATED_DATASETS/containers\",\n  \"state\": \"clean\",\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"type\": \"file\"\n}\n{\n  \"action\": \"status\",\n  \"bytesize\": 4026,\n  \"gitshasum\": \"f8c3632515b2138f6c1baddf848d85a54bab72e5\",\n  \"parentds\": \"/DATA/FEDERATED_DATASETS/containers\",\n  \"path\": \"/DATA/FEDERATED_DATASETS/containers/scripts/singularity_cmd\",\n  \"prev_gitshasum\": \"f8c3632515b2138f6c1baddf848d85a54bab72e5\",\n  \"refds\": \"/DATA/FEDERATED_DATASETS/containers\",\n  \"state\": \"clean\",\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"type\": \"file\"\n}\n{\n  \"action\": \"status\",\n  \"bytesize\": 259,\n  \"gitshasum\": \"